Roast.pet Weird Builder Projects

3 useful new projects after screening 25+ candidates and removing
previous finds.

1. Biometric-Pet-Identification by Grechka67
Project: github.com/Grechka67/Biometric-Pet-Identification
License: MIT repository; verify training-data/model rights separately
before commercial use.
Status: small current prototype, one commit.
Roast.pet step solved: pet-specific identity QA. The project combines
dog nose detection, face/nose identity embeddings, similarity search,
thresholds, and a safe unknown verdict. The author reports 98.4%
mAP@50 for the dog-nose detector and prototype dog identity results of
54% Rank-1 / 63% Rank-5 on a 2,000-dog subset, with 25 automated
tests. Stack: Python 3.11, PyTorch, YOLOv8, FAISS, FastAPI, Streamlit;
6 GB GPU or CPU.
How to reuse: invert its lost-pet workflow. Enroll the customer's
approved pet from several photos, then treat every generated
reaction/mouth state as a query. Combine whole-face similarity, nose
similarity and our landmark geometry before accepting a generated
state.
Work potentially replaced: first pet-specific embedding evaluation
harness, gallery handling, thresholding and evaluation scripts.
Integration: MEDIUM. Maintenance risk: MEDIUM.
Experiment: enroll 25 dogs from 3-5 source images each; create normal
transformations plus deliberately identity-drifted generations;
compare face-only, nose-only and combined scores for rejecting bad
generations.
Verdict: RESEARCH/FORK QA MECHANISM; verify model/data rights before shipping.

2. tscaps / @tscaps/engine by Franco Zanardi
Project: github.com/francozanardi/tscaps
License: engine MIT, templates MIT, studio AGPL-3.0. Use
engine/templates unless intentionally accepting the studio license.
Status: actively changing pre-1.0 project; 18 stars and 4 forks in the
surfaced repository.
Roast.pet step solved: browser caption preview and final caption
burn-in use the same HTML/CSS rendering surface. It accepts existing
structured caption data, so Roast.pet can feed HeadTTS timestamps
directly instead of retranscribing our own generated speech.
Install: npm install @tscaps/engine. Modern WebCodecs/Web Audio/Canvas browser.
Evidence: live local editor and published framework-agnostic engine;
export rasterizes the same DOM used during preview.
Work potentially replaced: caption compositor, DOM-to-video burn-in,
template plumbing and browser export glue.
Integration: LOW. Maintenance risk: MEDIUM because the API is pre-1.0;
pin a version/commit.
Experiment: feed 50 HeadTTS timestamp sets at 9:16, 1:1 and 16:9 and
pixel-check preview versus export, stressing long pet names,
punctuation, one-word punchlines and two-line captions.
Verdict: REUSE NOW / PIN VERSION.

3. meme-maker by SudoDEMON
Project: github.com/SudoDEMON/meme-maker
License: ISC.
Status: current personal toolkit surfaced recently.
Roast.pet step solved: the throwaway media glue around comedy
experiments: media conversion, captions, audio/stings, cropping,
MP4/WebM/GIF conversion and HTML-to-video/GIF/PNG/WebM through
Puppeteer. It also has a local web runner and caches preview clips.
Install: clone repository and run install.sh; uses yt-dlp, FFmpeg and
Node/Puppeteer.
Hardware: ordinary CPU.
Evidence: runnable scripts, install route, web UI and
browser-animation capture. It was explicitly built as a personal meme
toolkit rather than a large generic media framework.
How to reuse: keep Roast.pet's canonical renderer deterministic, but
use this as internal rapid-prototyping glue for podium variants,
stings, crops and social formats before promoting winning formats into
the production renderer.
Work potentially replaced: hundreds of lines of disposable
FFmpeg/Puppeteer wrapper code.
Integration: LOW. Maintenance risk: LOW.
Experiment: render one canonical podium scene as HTML, capture it with
the browser build script, add TTS and a sting, and generate 9:16 MP4
plus GIF. Compare setup time and determinism with our existing path.
Verdict: COPY/REUSE INTERNAL TOOLING; not core infrastructure.

TOP 5 THINGS WE SHOULD NOT BUILD OURSELVES
1. A pet-identity evaluation harness from scratch before benchmarking
the Biometric-Pet-Identification enrollment/query pattern.
2. Separate caption preview and final-render engines; tscaps
deliberately uses the same DOM/CSS surface for both.
3. Another transcription pass for speech Roast.pet generated itself;
feed HeadTTS timings directly to the caption engine.
4. One-off FFmpeg/Puppeteer wrappers for every meme/social experiment.
5. A giant editor for internal comedy experiments; use tiny tools for
rapid tests and keep production rendering deterministic.

Most surprising hobby project: Biometric-Pet-Identification.
Zero-star-scale work with real dog-nose detection, pet identity
metrics, tests and explicit limitations. Its lost-pet enrollment/query
model maps neatly to a generated-pet identity firewall.

Fastest integration win: tscaps engine. Roast.pet already has word
timings, so this can potentially remove the custom caption-compositor
layer immediately.

Repo/author worth following: Grechka67 / Biometric-Pet-Identification
for pet-specific computer-vision primitives with measured experiments.

Architectural implication: canonical source images can yield face
embedding + nose embedding + facial geometry; generated states yield
the same measurements; a weighted identity score then drives ACCEPT,
targeted RETRY or REJECT. Every production decision becomes additional
evaluation data for Roast.pet's identity firewall.
